The raw platform API is `PerformanceObserver`, which delivers performance entries as the browser records them:

```js
// Raw API: every long task over 50ms
new PerformanceObserver(list => {
  for (const entry of list.getEntries()) {
    console.log('long task', entry.duration, entry.attribution);
  }
}).observe({ type: 'longtask', buffered: true });

// Raw API: LCP keeps updating until the first interaction
new PerformanceObserver(list => {
  const entries = list.getEntries();
  const last = entries[entries.length - 1];
  console.log('LCP candidate', last.startTime, last.element);
}).observe({ type: 'largest-contentful-paint', buffered: true });
```

In production, use Google's **`web-vitals`** library rather than hand-rolling — it handles the genuinely fiddly parts: BFCache restores, tab visibility changes, which LCP candidate is final, and the INP percentile logic.

```js
import { onLCP, onINP, onCLS } from 'web-vitals/attribution';

function report({ name, value, rating, attribution }) {
  navigator.sendBeacon('/rum', JSON.stringify({
    name, value, rating,                    // 'good' | 'needs-improvement' | 'poor'
    target: attribution.interactionTarget    // or largestShiftTarget / lcpEntry
      ?? attribution.largestShiftTarget,
    route: location.pathname,
    conn: navigator.connection?.effectiveType,
  }));
}

onLCP(report); onINP(report); onCLS(report);
```

Two non-obvious details. Use `navigator.sendBeacon` (or `fetch` with `keepalive`) so the report survives the page unloading. And always send **route, device, and connection type** alongside the value — an aggregate number you can't segment is very hard to act on.
